---
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "How often do DMARC reports arrive?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Daily, one XML per provider per day. Gmail, Microsoft, and Yahoo each send their own, so volume scales with the providers your recipients use." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Why do I get reports from providers I do not use?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "The reports cover mail claiming to be your domain anywhere: spoofing attempts, forwarders, and recipients whose mailbox provider is that vendor." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What should I do with unknown source IPs?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Reverse DNS them first: many are legitimate services you forgot. The ones that fail authentication and are unknown are your spoofing candidates." } }
  ],
};
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

---
<BaseLayout
  schema={[faqSchema]}
  title="How to Read DMARC Reports (XML Made Simple)"
  description="How to read DMARC aggregate reports: the XML fields that matter, what pass/fail rows tell you, and how to turn reports into enforcement."
  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["Email Authentication", "/email-authentication/"], ["Read DMARC Reports"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Read DMARC Reports</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">How to Read DMARC Reports</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      DMARC aggregate reports are XML files mailed to your <code class="font-mono text-accent-soft">rua</code>
      address daily. Raw, they're unreadable; understood, they're a complete map of everything
      sending mail as your domain, legitimate or not.
    </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">DMARC aggregate XML fields that matter</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Example</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">source_ip</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">203.0.113.9</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Who actually sent mail claiming your domain</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">count</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">12</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Message volume from that source</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">spf / dkim results</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">pass / fail</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Which protocol carried authentication</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">disposition</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">none / quarantine / reject</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">What the receiver did with the mail</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-fields-that-matter">The fields that matter</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-to-read-a-row">How to read a row</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-weekly-rhythm">The weekly rhythm</a></li>
        <li><a href="#skip-the-xml-entirely">Skip the XML entirely</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="the-fields-that-matter">The fields that matter</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>report_metadata</strong>: who sent the report (Google, Microsoft...) and the date range it covers.</li>
        <li><strong>policy_published</strong>: the DMARC policy they saw, confirming your DNS is what you think it is.</li>
        <li><strong>Each record row</strong>: <code>source_ip</code> (who sent), <code>count</code> (how many messages), <code>header_from</code> (the From domain shown to users), and <code>auth_results</code> (raw SPF/DKIM outcomes).</li>
        <li><strong>policy_evaluated</strong>: what the provider actually did: <code>disposition</code> none/quarantine/reject, plus aligned DKIM/SPF verdicts.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="how-to-read-a-row">How to read a row</h2>
      <p>
        Alignment is the story. A row with SPF pass but DMARC fail usually means the sender is
        legitimate but not aligned (a SaaS sending from its own domain); fix it by configuring that
        service to send as yours. A row with everything failing from an IP you don't recognize is
        spoofing, and your reason to move past <code>p=none</code>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-weekly-rhythm">The weekly rhythm</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>Collect the attachments (XML or zipped XML) each day or week.</li>
        <li>Group rows by source IP and count: your top senders surface immediately.</li>
        <li>Fix every legitimate source until pass rates sit at 100%.</li>
        <li>Step the policy up: quarantine, then reject.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="skip-the-xml-entirely">Skip the XML entirely</h2>
      <p>
        Drop any report file into the <a href="/dmarc-report-analyzer/">free DMARC report analyzer</a>:
        summary cards, pass rates, top sources, and the full records table, parsed in your browser.
      </p>

      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/dmarc/">DMARC: the complete guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dmarc-record-generator/">DMARC record generator</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How often do DMARC reports arrive?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Daily, one XML per provider per day. Gmail, Microsoft, and Yahoo each send their own, so volume scales with the providers your recipients use.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why do I get reports from providers I do not use?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The reports cover mail claiming to be your domain anywhere: spoofing attempts, forwarders, and recipients whose mailbox provider is that vendor.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What should I do with unknown source IPs?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Reverse DNS them first: many are legitimate services you forgot. The ones that fail authentication and are unknown are your spoofing candidates.</p></details></div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
